Admission control decides whether to accept incoming work.
A busy [service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies) can accept requests as capacity frees up and gracefully reject the rest.
[Google SRE](https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/) warns against stopping all traffic when a backend becomes overloaded.

## Worked example: refuse while the permit is held

A semaphore tracks permits, or slots for concurrent work.
In [Effect](/lore/effect-basics) 4.1.4, `withPermit` waits for one permit and releases it when the wrapped effect finishes.
`withPermitsIfAvailable` makes a different choice: it returns `Option.none` without running the work when the requested permits are unavailable.

This small example checks both outcomes:

```ts title="A local admission probe"
import { Effect, Semaphore } from "effect";

export const admissionProbe = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const capacity = yield* Semaphore.make(1);
  const work = Effect.succeed("served");
  const busy = yield* capacity.withPermit(
    capacity.withPermitsIfAvailable(1)(work),
  );
  const recovered = yield* capacity.withPermitsIfAvailable(1)(work);
  return { busy, recovered };
});
```

1. The outer `withPermit` holds the only permit.
2. The inner attempt returns `Option.none` without running `work`.
3. The outer effect finishes and releases the permit. The next attempt returns `Option.some("served")`.

This sequential probe checks local permit behavior.
It does not test concurrent load.
The single permit makes the example small; it is not a production capacity recommendation.

## Carry the refusal to the caller

The semaphore returns a local result.
It does not define an HTTP response or a caller retry policy.
The caller must decide how to handle `Option.none`.

Rejection still costs resources.
Google SRE describes client-side throttling that caps outgoing traffic when many requests are rejected.
It also limits retries to avoid amplifying overload.
A local capacity check and caller cooperation address different parts of that problem.

Read [backpressure](/lore/backpressure-reaches-the-producer) for producer cooperation, then [retry budgets](/lore/retry-load-needs-a-budget) for repeated attempts.
Use [the durability skill](/skills/durability) to review the surrounding system.
